A||||16||btoth||Time for a new engine.  ????'s||||9||
Z||000000||btoth||04-02-2001||02:52 PM||||Well, my engine (2.8) started knocking really bad (loud!) two days ago.  I think it must have been doing this for some time now, but I mistook it for an exhaust leak.  But now it is obviously coming from the engine.  This wouldn't be <B>so</B> bad except that I just had this engine rebuilt from the heads up a year ago this week.  And from what I've been told, it's probably in need of lower end bearings, too.<P>I was looking at new cars this week but, honestly, I can't seem to find a new car for under $20k that I would like to pay monthly payments for. (I turn 20 this month and currently have no job till the summer because of college.)  And if I were to buy a used car, I'd rather just keep my Fiero and plop a new engine in it.  (Which I think I should have just done the first time the engine took a dump on me.)<P>So, if I were to get a new engine (not a junkyard engine) I have some questions:<P>1.)  My best bet would be a 3.1 right?  Direct replacement for the 2.8 as I understand.<BR>2.)  Long-block or short-block?  Long-blocks come with heads, cylinders, valves, etc. already assembled properly and short-blocks don't, right?  Need some clarification.<BR>3.)  Should it be easy enough to get the engine and have it installed through any GM dealership service center?  Or should it be Pontiac specific?<BR>4.)  If anyone else has had this done... around how much $$$ am I looking at?  $2k?  or more?  (A new engine in my friend's Baretta was around $1800, but of course, with a Fiero...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )<BR>5.)  Any other idea's, tips?<P><BR>Thanks for the space, this car has been an endless stress these past couple weeks but I don't want to give up on it yet.<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 04-02-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||Taijiguy||04-02-2001||03:57 PM||||You might be able to get by with just replacing the rod bearings. Here's the readers digest version of the process:<BR>1. Pull the pan to gain access to the bottom end. You can do this with the motor in place.<BR>2. One at a time, remove the rod caps and inspect the bearings and crank journals. (remember to note the cap orientation, it needs to go back on the way it came off, unless it was on wrong to begin with LOL)<BR>3. use 1000 grit wet/or dry sandpaper to smooth the crank journal. If it's badly scored, you can go from 400, to 600, and then polish it with the 1000. Use oil to lubricate the paper.<BR>4. Wipe the journal really well, and install the upper bearing *DRY*<BR>5. Install the lower bearing into the cap, again, dry, and install the cap with a piece of plasti-gage and torque the cap to specs.<BR>Pull the cap and make sure the clearance is within specs. If not, you'll need to get a bearing for that journal that sized to compensate for the wear.<BR>6. Repeat the plasti-gage steps if another bearing is installed.<BR>7. Remove the plasti-gage, and grease up the bearings with an oil soluble grease (I like white lithium)and tighten down the caps<BR>8. Repeat this procedure for the remaining rods.<P>It may not be an entirely bad idea to check the mains when you're down there. They can be replaced too if necessary, you just have to be creative about getting the new ones in. I'd probably put in a new oil pump too just for the insurance.<BR>I had two rod bearings go bad, that motor slapped for almost a year before I finally tore into it. The two rearmost bearings completely thrashed, but fortunately, the crank was in grreat shape, I did the above steps to the crank, and when I finshed, it was quiet as can be. Perfect. So you may not be completly screwed or in need of a new motor.<BR>Good luck!<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||04-02-2001||04:10 PM||||I agree with everthing there about the bearing replacement except the sandpaper.  I'd be a bit leary of taking sandpaper to my crank.  Just the potential for sanding residue to get in the oil passages, if nothing else.  Still, I'm sure it can be done if you're careful enough.  I wouldn't do it, myself.  Just my .02<P>But checking the clearance and installing new bearings (and hi-volume oil pump) would be a good idea before biting the bullet on a new engine.  ||||reg||3
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-02-2001||04:39 PM||||Well, I did mine, and it worked great. Besides, so what if there's the potential of a tiny amount of residue entering the crank oil passage? It's so miniscule and will flush out in a matter of seconds, likely causing no damage anyway. Besides, there's probably at least one spun bearing there already anyway- he's talking about having to replace the motor as it is, not like it can be made worse. Shoot, shove a little scrap of a rag into the journal opening if it's that much worry, no risk then.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fyerstarter||04-02-2001||05:01 PM||||I think that I may have the same problem with my car. The knocking or slapping appears to be related to the amount of gas I give it rather than rpm's. For example if I'm cruising at 3500 rpms with no gas there will be no noise, then If I give it a bit of gas the knocking will start and as soon as I let off it will stop again. This led me to believe that it was probably an exhaust leak. But when you pop the decklid the noise is definitely coming from the vicinity of the engine so unless it's bad header gaskets I may be mistaken. Basically I was wondering if it is bad rod-bearings would the noise be related to the throttle rather than rpm's? I know when you step on the gas the rpms rise but even if I give it just a little gas the noise starts. Also how big of a job is it to replace the bearings? Is this something that your average shade tree mechanic can complete in a weekend? Any special tools needed? Thanks for the info  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||04-02-2001||06:57 PM||||You're correct that it's not related to RPM's. What you described is exactly what I had with mine. I found mine by pulling the head, as I was *hoping* it was valvetrain, but I knew that wasn't really it. That was just wishful thinking. I put a dial indicator on each piston and found number 5 and 6 with about .011 play! Fortunately it was all in bearing wear and no real damage to the crank. <BR>It's probably a long afternoon job. Supposedly the front pan bolt is a pain to get to, but I didn't really have a big problem getting to it. If you're fairly well equipped with a good stock of tools and can get the car in the air high enough to move around a little, it shouldn't be too bad. <BR>Besides, what could be a better day? Cold beers, and the sun shining on your feet as you lay under your Fiero!||||reg||6
Z||000006||fyerstarter||04-02-2001||11:33 PM||||DOH! I was hoping you would say "yeah thats just an exhaust leak" :P Well thanks for the info, at least I know what it is now. Sounds like a pretty common problem. Does anyone know any reputable repair shops in southern california that specialize in fieros? I could prob do it myself but seeing as I live in an apartment complex it might just be easier to take it to a shop rather than having my feet run over while working on it :P||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||04-03-2001||11:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>around how much $$$ am I looking at?  $2k?  or more?  (A new engine in my friend's Baretta was around $1800, but of course, with a Fiero...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <BR>If you are looking at a GM dealer to source your new crate motor, put all the Fiero goodies on it and install it in your car,you can AT LEAST double that $1800. That maybe will pay for the crate longblock, but I doubt it. If you have the resources to do a swap, then pull your engine yourself and get the bottom end rebuilt.  I had my engine replaced with a rebuilt and it wound up costing me $2400 plus about $600 worth of optional b.s. (Powdercoating, high output ignition, that kinda stuff) and that was through a shop in a rural area with a rep for fair prices for quality work.||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||04-03-2001||12:31 PM||||<B>Stimpy</B>  How long did it take the shop to install the new engine??||||reg||1
