Z||000013||Leadfoot||07-06-2001||03:43 PM||||I bought a long block from ATK for $1500.  That includes shipping and handling, gaskets and a 3 year unlimited mile warranty.  It took me every night for 2 months to get it done but it is in and working fine (knock on wood).  Most of the work involved replacing bolts and hoses that broke off from the old motor.  However, if I had to do it over I would have probably done the 3.4 swap.  This is what I would have done if I new about this forum much sooner.  Heck how was I suppose to know that so many people are interested in restoring the greatest GM invention....the Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||btoth||07-06-2001||06:16 PM||||Got a new long-block installed in May... running great now!!  But thanks for the offer.  <P>(I was surprised to see this thread dug up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>Mine was $1900 (for the one with the performance cam and other better internal parts).  $900 labor, and only took a week to do.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Rex||07-06-2001||06:31 PM||||I believe I'd just go with a junkyard 3.1 with low millage.Shop around,look in trading papers, etc.: most places will warranty the engine also. What time frame are you looking at? I'm almost sure forum members will assist you in your search also. good luck!!||||reg||1
Z||000016||SteveJ||07-06-2001||08:01 PM||||A mild rod knock shows up when the engine is under load. Just stand next to it and hit the throttle. You should hear it then as the engine speeds up and maybe again as it slows. A main bearing knock is deeper in tone and is more of a thump sound which you can hear at an even idle. The main bearing knock comes from deep in the engine and is hard to locate.||||reg||1
